ets were meant to support to  support Lance Armstrong who's this  gentleman and he's a spokesperson for  bristol-myers squibb and was cured  partly by these platen drugs as have  been many people and so this is now I  think about the third best-selling  anti-cancer medicine the most you know  best mostly used and again while not  perfect it's really cured a large number  of people and so it was an accident it  was an accident but it's been really  really a successful medicine yes well  there's a joke that he call it that the  cancer has been cured in e.coli many  times so you know you can do things  happen to any coli but of course you  always wonder about the human  applications and something that is that  that is so that affects cell division so  much you'd think about what it might do  to a cell that's dividing all the time  which is what a cancer cell does it  would more specifically damage those  than one that wasn't dividing all the  time nobody else okay so the way that  just real quickly because I'm going a  little over the way we think cisplatin  works is that it goes in and binds to  DNA and it kinks the DNA the way I've  shown so what actually happens is is  that the chlorides these two chlorides  come off all right chlorides come on and  it binds to a piece of DNA so if I'm DNA  the cisplatin binds to me and when it  does that it makes it sort of kink and  twist and that causes a lot of problems  with the copying of DNA and with the  reading of DNA and with the repair and  again as the question addressed because  tumor cells are dividing more often than  normal cells it's relatively more  damaging to a cancer cell than to a  healthy cell so that's the way it works  but that's there's an awful lot of other  biology and chemistry behind that rather  glib description so the summary none of  my second part is that platinum  compounds like cisplatin were  accidentally discovered to be very  effective medicines against cancer but  again they're not perfect and there's  still a lot of work that needs to be  done about them so if you'll just give  me like a minute at the end I just want  to say a few things about about  chemistry  because you've seen a couple different  kinds of science today and I think while  I admire all fields of science I think  there's you know amazing biology and as  as Rosenberg show there's really great  stuff that happens when you're aware of  both you know biology and physics right  at the end of the day I'm a chemist and  so so I think it's a fair question what  I think is the good thing about  chemistry so the thing there are two  things the first thing that I think is  really cool about chemistry is that we  get to make stuff and and so in a lot of  kinds of science including some kinds of  chemistry we try to figure out what's  true about nature what's true about the  world but chemistry more than some other  kinds of science gets to invent our own  new stuff you know make new materials  and make new molecules that have never  been made before and that is abuzz for a  lot of us you know that's that's pretty  cool and so when I say creative I don't  mean like creativity and experiment  design I mean actually making stuff and  that that's what attracts a lot of  chemists to chemistry and finally for me  quite frankly other there's this thing  that I like to call the Goldilocks  effect which is that I really liked  science a lot but for me biology is too  big biology is very complicated you know  there are lots of big molecules are  doing a lot of stuff and for me that was  that was big physics is either too small  or too big depending on you know if like  atomic physics is really tiny and then  astrophysics is really big so you like  Goldilocks right too hot too cold just  right for me science was too big too  small just right so i think there's  certainly a room for lots of kinds of  science I certainly would never say  anything bad about my colleagues in  science but for me for me these two 